Issues.Body:
### Which @angular/\* package(s) are relevant/related to the feature request?

core, compiler, compiler-cli

### Description

Angular applies the union of an element's classes from the component's host, its other directives, and the template. With utility CSS such as Tailwind, conflicting classes both stay, and the stylesheet picks the winner:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'ui-button',
  host: {class: 'px-4 py-2'},
  template: '<ng-content />',
})
export class UiButton {}
```

```html
<ui-button class="px-2">Save</ui-button>
<!-- Today: class="px-4 py-2 px-2". px-2 may or may not win. -->
<!-- Wanted: class="py-2 px-2". -->
```

Libraries work around it with an input aliased to `class` and a computed host `[class]`, as spartan-ng does. That can't see `[class.x]` bindings or other directives' classes, and each library reimplements it.

### Proposed solution

An app provides a resolver, and a `(class)` binding resolves an element's classes with it. A prototype is implemented and tested: https://github.com/Flarette/angular/pull/5. Reference: https://github.com/Flarette/angular/blob/feat/class-resolver/docs/class-resolver.md

#### Provide a resolver

```ts
bootstrapApplication(App, {providers: [provideClassResolver(twMerge)]});

// Angular calls it with one string per source, lowest priority first:
twMerge('px-4 py-2' /* component */, '' /* directives */, 'px-2' /* template */); // 'py-2 px-2'
```

Any `(...classes: string[]) => string` fits. Without a provider, classes merge as they do today. A library can scope its own resolver with `viewProviders`.

#### Resolve a component's host

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'ui-button',
  host: {class: 'px-4 py-2 bg-primary', '(class)': 'classes'},
  template: '<ng-content />',
})
export class UiButton {
  readonly disabled = input(false);
  protected readonly classes: ClassCallback = (twMerge) => twMerge(this.disabled() && 'bg-muted');
}
```

```html
<ui-button class="px-2" />
<!-- class="py-2 bg-primary px-2" -->

<ui-button class="px-2 bg-danger" [disabled]="true" />
<!-- class="py-2 px-2 bg-danger": the template still wins over the component's extra bg-muted -->
```

#### Resolve in a template

```html
<ui-button class="px-2" (class)="(twMerge) => twMerge('shadow-lg', busy() && 'opacity-50')" />
```

`(class)="F"` is shorthand for `(attr.class)="$event.resolve((F)($event.resolver))"`. The full form gives a handler the classes of each source:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-toolbar',
  imports: [UiButton],
  template: `<ui-button class="px-2" (attr.class)="resolveButton($event)" />`,
})
export class Toolbar {
  readonly busy = signal(false);
  readonly compact = signal(false);

  protected resolveButton(event: ClassResolveEvent) {
    // event.sources, after UiButton's own host resolver ran:
    // [['py-2', 'bg-primary'], [], ['px-2']]
    if (this.compact()) {
      event.resolve('px-1 py-1'); // exact classes, no resolver
    } else if (this.busy()) {
      event.resolve(event.resolver('opacity-50')); // twMerge with an extra class
    }
    // No resolve() call: the element keeps the classes as collected.
  }
}
```

The shorthand must return what its resolver returns. Both of these are compile errors:

```html
<div (class)="(twMerge) => 'px-2'"></div>
<div (class)="onClick"></div>
```

#### Resolve every element

```ts
bootstrapApplication(App, {providers: [provideClassResolver(twMerge, withAutoResolution())]});

@Component({
  // Off for this component's template.
  viewProviders: [provideClassResolver(twMerge)],
})
export class LegacyWidget {}
```

Every element that a component or directive matches resolves without a binding.

#### Several resolvers on one element

Priority: template > directives > component. Every resolver runs, lowest priority first, and each gets the previous result as its sources.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'ui-button',
  host: {class: 'px-4 bg-primary', '(class)': 'resolve'},
  template: '<ng-content />',
})
export class UiButton {
  protected readonly resolve: ClassCallback = (twMerge) => twMerge('rounded');
}

@Directive({
  selector: '[tone]',
  host: {class: 'bg-gray-100', '(class)': 'resolve'},
})
export class Tone {
  protected readonly resolve: ClassCallback = (twMerge) => twMerge('text-gray-900');
}
```

```html
<ui-button tone class="px-2 bg-white" (class)="(twMerge) => twMerge('shadow')" />
```

```text
sources        component 'px-4 bg-primary'   directives 'bg-gray-100'    template 'px-2 bg-white'
1. UiButton    component 'rounded'           directives ''               template 'px-2 bg-white'
2. Tone        component 'rounded'           directives 'text-gray-900'  template 'px-2 bg-white'
3. template    class="rounded text-gray-900 px-2 bg-white shadow"
```

Each resolver's extra classes join its own source, so a component's extras are defaults that directives and the template override.

#### Cost

About 2 µs or less per resolved element in dev mode, not counting the resolver. Apps that don't use it don't bundle it.

#### Breaking changes

```ts
@Directive({selector: '[dir]'})
export class Dir {
  // `<div dir (class)="...">` no longer listens to this output. NG8120 warns.
  readonly class = output<string>();
}
```

```ts
@HostListener('class') onClass() {} // Now a compile error.
```

Outputs named `class` are rare. Until #71661, such an output also dropped the element's `[class]` binding, so apps are unlikely to depend on one.

### Alternatives considered

Today's alternative is the userland workaround above. The open design choices, with why the prototype chose what it did:

- **All attributes, not only `class`.** `aria-describedby` and other token lists have the same problem:

  ```ts
  provideAttributeResolvers({class: twMerge, 'aria-describedby': joinIds});
  ```

  The prototype resolves only `class`, because each attribute needs its own merge rule. Other `(attr.*)` names stay ordinary event bindings, so the namespace is still free for this.

- **Styles.** A `(style)` counterpart that resolves conflicting style declarations. Not designed yet.
- **The shorthand's shape.** Today the callback gets only the bound resolver. It could also get the sources:

  ```html
  <!-- Today -->
  <div (class)="(twMerge) => twMerge('px-2')"></div>
  <!-- With the sources as a second argument -->
  <div (class)="(twMerge, sources) => twMerge(...sources[0], 'px-2')"></div>
  <!-- The first prototype: the callback got the sources and returned the classes,
       which a component method merged -->
  <div (class)="(sources) => merge(sources, 'px-2')"></div>
  ```

  Why the prototype chose today's shape:
  - **The callback returns what the resolver returns.** An earlier version used a handler's return value as the classes. No other Angular event does that, the value wasn't type-checked, and a later statement in a handler could replace the classes silently. The result is now an opaque type that only the resolver creates, so the compiler rejects `(twMerge) => 'px-2'`, and a cast fails in dev mode.
  - **The sources stay in the full form.** A handler that needs them uses `(attr.class)` and `$event.sources`. The shorthand stays one call for the common case.
  - **Extras join the caller's source.** Appending them after every source let a component's extras beat the consumer's classes.
  - **The resolver takes and returns strings:** `(...classes: string[]) => string`. A string is what a DOM attribute takes natively, for any attribute, so the same contract can serve a resolver for any attribute. `class` adds one step: Angular first reads extras and `resolve()` values the way a `[class]` binding does, so arrays, Sets, and object maps work, and `false` adds nothing. `twMerge` and a shadcn `cn` fit unchanged. A contract that takes `[class]` values fails `twMerge`'s types, and declaration merging for the extras' type breaks when two libraries augment it differently.

- **Several resolvers.** Every resolver runs, and each gets the previous result back in its sources. Letting one resolver replace the others depended on `imports` order and dropped a library's logic silently. Letting a lower resolver delegate upward let its appended classes beat the consumer's.
- **Naming.** `(class)` and `(attr.class)` collide with outputs named `class`. A name such as `(class.resolve)` avoids the breaking change, but it loses the short form, and `(class.x)` reads like a modifier, as in `(keydown.enter)`.
- **Automatic resolution's reach.** It could resolve every element. The prototype chose not to: every element would pay the extra cost, and an element that no component or directive matches has only the template's classes, so it has no conflict to resolve. A template that needs a merge there can import `twMerge` and bind `[class]` to its result. Resolving every element would trade explicitness and performance for consistency and less boilerplate.


### RFC

The class resolution is a special case of the  #67519, Angular has no API for resolving host conflicts and relies on defaults (classes join, Id's override). Other frameworks avoid it by not having the selectors, in angular it is a friction point. The full feature could resolve any attribute, with shorthands such as `(class)` for the common cases or cases with special defaults ex: (class) accepting same types as [class]. Before proceeding with the implementation i want to hear the opinion of the maintainers and users if this API fits the angular vision

In the `.d.ts` statics (`ɵmod`, `ɵcmp`/`ɵdir` `hostDirectives`, `ngAcceptInputType_*`), `ngtsc` writes every reference through its reference emitter, and the declaration transform's `ImportManager` gives each one a fresh `import * as iN`. `ngp` reused the user's own import binding instead. So barrels, `paths` aliases, deprecated re-export aliases, two imports of the same exported name, namespace-qualified transform types, and unresolvable aliased imports all came out with the user's specifier, local name, or namespace.

This change matches `ngtsc`'s three paths:
1. **`toR3Reference` with `ImportFlags.ForceNewImport`.** This covers the `ɵmod` lists and `hostDirectives`. The type side now goes through the import strategy (`ApfImportStrategy` / `PrefixImportStrategy`) as a fresh import of the declaring module, named by its exported name.
2. **Declaration-only emission (`resolvedToTypeTupleElement` + `routeEntityNameThroughImportManager`).** Here `ngtsc` keeps the user's specifier but still writes `iN.<exported name>`. `ngtsc` runs declaration-only emission as local compilation either way, so `ngp` now gives the same output with or without `compilationMode: 'experimental-local'`.
3. **`translateTypeReference` for transform parameter types.** Each type name is resolved to its declaration, following named, default, type-only, and namespace imports and type-only re-exports, and then re-emitted through the strategy.

`normalizeDtsNamedImportsInStaticDeclarations` in the ngp test normalizer existed only to hide this difference, so this change removes it.

### Input

```ts
// lib/dir.ts
@Directive({selector: '[dir]'})
export class Dir {}

// lib/index.ts
export {Dir} from './dir';

// test.ts
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';
import {Dir} from './lib/index';

@NgModule({imports: [Dir], exports: [Dir]})
export class M {}
```

### Output Before Fix (`ngp`)

```ts
import { Dir } from './lib/index';
import * as i0 from '@angular/core';
export declare class M {
    static ɵmod: i0.ɵɵNgModuleDeclaration<M, never, [typeof Dir], [typeof Dir]>;
    // ...
}
```

### Output After Fix (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc`)

```ts
import * as i0 from '@angular/core';
import * as i1 from './lib/dir';
export declare class M {
    static ɵmod: i0.ɵɵNgModuleDeclaration<M, never, [typeof i1.Dir], [typeof i1.Dir]>;
    // ...
}
```

With `_useHostForImportGeneration` (Google3's `fileNameToModuleName`), the specifier is `root/lib/dir` on both sides.

### Other patterns

| Input | Before (`ngp`) | After (`ngp` = `ngtsc`) |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| `import {Other} from 'lib-dir'` via `paths` | `typeof Other` from `'lib-dir'` | `typeof i1.Other` from `'lib-dir'` (`'root/lib/dir'` with the host) |
| `export {RealDir as OldDir}` + `hostDirectives: [OldDir]` | `directive: typeof OldDir` | `directive: typeof i1.RealDir` |
| `import {TaskDetail} from './a/task'` + `import {TaskDetail as TaskDetailOld} from './b/task'` | `typeof TaskDetail, typeof i1.TaskDetail` | `typeof i1.TaskDetail, typeof i2.TaskDetail` |
| `import * as api from './api'`; `@Input({transform: (v: api.Item[]) => …})` | `ngAcceptInputType_items: api.Item[]` | `ngAcceptInputType_items: i1.Item[]` |
| Declaration-only, unresolvable `import {AwesomechartsModule as Ng2AwesomechartsModule} from 'ng2-awesomecharts'` | `typeof Ng2AwesomechartsModule` | `typeof i1.AwesomechartsModule` from `'ng2-awesomecharts'` |

### Behavior changes to review

- **JS:** the compiler builds `ɵɵHostDirectivesFeature` from the type reference, so for a deprecated alias the JS goes from `ɵɵHostDirectivesFeature([OldDir])` to `ɵɵHostDirectivesFeature([i1.RealDir])` plus `import * as i1 from './lib'`. That is exactly `ngtsc`'s JS. Preprocessor goldens are updated accordingly.
- **Known regression in inferred types:** TypeScript spells an inferred type through any namespace import in scope. Where this change adds an `import * as iN` for a module, an inferred type from that module is now printed `i1.Thing`, where `ngtsc` (and `ngp` before) print `import("./lib/dir").Thing`. `ngtsc` avoids this only because it adds its imports after TypeScript's declaration emit. `ngp` can't do the same, since it hands `.ng.ts` source to an unmodified `tsc`. A spec pins this behind `TODO(D4)`.
- **Two `ngtsc` quirks copied for raw parity**, each producing an invalid `.d.ts`: in declaration-only tuples, `import Dflt from` becomes `typeof i1.Dflt`, and `ReturnType<typeof ns.X.forRoot>` becomes `ReturnType<typeof i1.ns.X.forRoot>`.

Remaining `TODO(parity)`s:
- transforms declared in a `.d.ts` whose types reference other symbols;
- deeper qualified transform types (`ns.Inner.Foo`);
- local-compilation JS for `hostDirectives` (pre-existing);
- package re-export aliases in value positions, e.g. component `dependencies`.

> [!NOTE]
> This conflicts with #71876 (generated namespace alias collisions) in `preprocessor/src/output_ast_printer.ts`, since both change how namespace aliases are allocated. Whichever lands second needs a rebase.

When a deferrable import was still referenced in a type position, `ngp` added a `type` modifier to each of its specifiers so the downstream `tsc` would drop it from the JS. TypeScript's declaration emit keeps that modifier. So the `.d.ts` read `import { type Heavy }` where `ngtsc` keeps the user's clause, `import { Heavy }`.

`ngtsc` removes the import only in its JS transform (`IvyTransformationVisitor.visitOtherNode`), so its declaration emit sees the original clause. `ngp` mostly doesn't need the rewrite. Once the decorator is stripped, nothing references the binding as a value, and `tsc` already drops such an import from the JS, with or without `isolatedModules`.

This change leaves the import as written, except for specifiers the output would still read as a value:
- **An aliased specifier (`{Heavy as H}`).** The `ɵsetClassMetadataAsync` callback names its parameter after the exported name, while the copied decorator reads the local alias.
- **Under `emitDecoratorMetadata`, a binding `tsc` serializes into `design:*` metadata.** `ng-analyze` flags these bindings while it classifies import references, mirroring the checker's `markDecoratorAliasReferenced` / `getEntityNameForDecoratorMetadata` for the decorators `ngp` leaves in the output. To know which those are, the Angular DI parameter decorators (`@Inject`, `@Optional`, …) are now part of the class's removal spans in `ng-analyze`, rather than being removed separately in `buildDeps`.
- **Under `verbatimModuleSyntax`,** the old rewrite is kept unchanged.

JS output is unchanged in every mode.

### Input

```ts
// heavy.ts
@Component({selector: 'heavy', template: ''})
export class Heavy {}
export interface HeavyConfig { a: string; }

// test.ts (onlyExplicitDeferDependencyImports: true)
import {Component, viewChild} from '@angular/core';
import {Heavy, type HeavyConfig} from './heavy';

@Component({
  selector: 'c',
  template: '@defer (name block1) { <heavy #h/> }',
  deferredImports: {block1: [Heavy]},
})
export class C {
  readonly h = viewChild<Heavy>('h');
  config?: HeavyConfig;
}
```

### Output Before Fix (`ngp`)

```ts
// test.d.ts
import { type Heavy, type HeavyConfig } from './heavy';
```

### Output After Fix (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc`)

```ts
// test.d.ts
import { Heavy, type HeavyConfig } from './heavy';
```

`test.js` is byte-identical before and after. It has no static import of `./heavy`, only the dynamic `import('./heavy')`.

In three cases the `.d.ts` still shows the added `type`. Dropping it there would make the deferred module load eagerly in the JS. Each case has a `TODO(parity)`:
- **Aliased specifiers.** `ngtsc` emits the same mismatched callback but deletes the import.
- **Bindings serialized under `emitDecoratorMetadata`.** `ngtsc` deletes the import and leaves `__metadata("design:type", Heavy)` dangling.
- **Anything under `verbatimModuleSyntax`.**

`ngtsc`'s `ImportManager` names the `n`th generated namespace import `i<n>`, then moves it to `i<n>_1`, `i<n>_2`, … while that name is in the source file's `identifiers` table or has already been generated. The rule comes from `createGenerateUniqueIdentifierHelper` in `check_unique_identifier_name.ts`. TypeScript's parser fills that table with every identifier token at any depth, so even a nested arrow parameter named `i0` moves the `@angular/core` import to `i0_1`.

`ngp` always used `i0`, `i1`, …. Its output differed from `ngtsc`'s, and a top-level `const i0` in user code broke compilation outright: the generated references resolved to the user's binding.

This change:
1. Collects the names in the file that have the shape of a generated alias. This happens in `ng-analyze` (`namespace_alias_conflicts`), from oxc's semantic node list, mirroring the TypeScript parser's `identifiers` interning. The names are reported as `AnalysisResult.namespaceAliasConflicts`.
2. Applies `ngtsc`'s uniqueness rule in `ExpressionPrinter` whenever it allocates a namespace alias, including `@angular/core`'s. The `.ng.ts`, and so both the JS and the `.d.ts`, use `ngtsc`'s names.
3. Stops reusing a namespace import that's already in the file. `ngtsc` never reuses an existing import for generated code (`presetImportManagerForceNamespaceImports` sets `disableOriginalSourceFileReuse: true`), so a user-written `import * as i0 from '@angular/core'` now gets a separate `i0_1` import, as in `ngtsc`.

### Input

```ts
import {Injectable} from '@angular/core';

export interface Incident { stage: string; }

@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})
export class ImpactService {
  same(a: Incident[]) {
    return a.every((i0: Incident) => i0.stage === a[0].stage);
  }
}
```

### Output Before Fix (`ngp`)

```ts
// test.d.ts
import * as i0 from '@angular/core';
export declare class ImpactService {
    same(a: Incident[]): boolean;
    static ɵfac: i0.ɵɵFactoryDeclaration<ImpactService, never>;
    static ɵprov: i0.ɵɵInjectableDeclaration<any>;
}
```

### Output After Fix (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc`)

```ts
// test.d.ts
import * as i0_1 from '@angular/core';
export declare class ImpactService {
    same(a: Incident[]): boolean;
    static ɵfac: i0_1.ɵɵFactoryDeclaration<ImpactService, never>;
    static ɵprov: i0_1.ɵɵInjectableDeclaration<any>;
}
```

The JS output changes the same way: `import * as i0_1 from '@angular/core'` and `i0_1.ɵɵdefineInjectable(…)`.

### Top-level collision

```ts
import {Injectable} from '@angular/core';

const i0 = 1;

@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})
export class TopService {
  v = i0;
}
```

- **Before (`ngp`):** fails to compile with `Property 'ɵsetClassMetadata' does not exist on type '1'.`
- **After (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc`):** compiles, with `import * as i0_1 from '@angular/core'` in both the JS and the `.d.ts`, and `this.v = i0` left untouched.

Remaining differences:
- **JSDoc identifiers:** `ngtsc` also counts identifiers that appear only in JSDoc; oxc doesn't put those in the AST. There's a `TODO(parity)`.
- **Numbering:** `ngp` uses one numbering for both the JS and the `.d.ts`, where `ngtsc` numbers them independently. This predates the change.

> [!NOTE]
> This conflicts with #71878 (`.d.ts` reference emission) in `preprocessor/src/output_ast_printer.ts`, since both change how namespace aliases are allocated. Whichever lands second needs a rebase.
